# Partita No. 5 in G major, BWV 829: Tempo di Minuetta (J. S. Bach)
#
# Character: the fifth movement of the partita, a minuet in name and
# metre but a famous rhythmic puzzle. Written in 3/4, it is built from
# broken chords in eighths in which the melody note falls on every third
# eighth, so the ear hears two beats of three eighths (6/8) against the
# written three quarters, and the left hand crosses over the right to
# play those notes. Only the hemiola cadences (bars 11-12, 31-32, 51-52)
# settle into plain quarters. Light and graceful, never heavy.
#
# Time signature: 3/4, 52 bars, no upbeat. Two halves, each repeated:
# A bars 1-12, B bars 13-52 (Mutopia 55title-hub.mid / 55hub.ly: the
# fifth movement, "5. Tempo di Minuetta"; a comment in the title file
# calls it the Sarabande, but the score and its header are the
# Minuetta). The MIDI plays each half once; the repeats are written out
# below.
#
# Bars as performed (play 1-12 1-12 13-52 13-52):
#   A first time 1-12, A repeat 13-24,
#   B first time 25-64, B repeat 65-104.
#   Score bar Bx (13-52) is performed bar x+12 the first time, x+52 the
#   repeat.
#
# Tempo: quarter = 150 (dotted half 50), with both repeats about 2:07.
# Confirmed track times: Angela Hewitt (2018 recording, AHCD030, her own
# listing) 1:51; Andras Schiff (live, Amazon listing) 2:23. Both
# timings only make sense with the repeats taken (without them they
# would mean quarter = 84 or less, far too slow for a minuet), giving
# about quarter = 169 for Hewitt and 131 for Schiff. 150 lies between:
# a flowing one-in-a-bar that keeps the cross-rhythm clear on the chip
# (eighths of 12 frames).
#
# Structure (Bach-Gesellschaft text as set by Mutopia; my reading):
#   1-4    G major, the crossing-hands figure on the tonic chord;
#          a trilled cadence figure in bar 4.
#   5-10   falling sequence through the circle of fifths.
#   11-12  hemiola, cadence in D major. (Repeat.)
#   13-16  D major, rising; cadence in E minor at 16.
#   17-20  the same to A minor (20).
#   21-30  sequences through B minor and E minor, the melody high.
#   31-32  hemiola, cadence in E minor.
#   33-40  over a pedal: A and D alternating in the bass with the figure
#          above (the dominant of G returning).
#   41-44  scales and broken chords in G: the home key arrives.
#   45-50  falling sequence back to the tonic.
#   51-52  hemiola, final cadence in G (fermata). (Repeat.)
#
# Plan: as on a two-manual harpsichord, each half is played full the
# first time and echoed on the second manual for the repeat. In the B
# repeat the full sound comes back at score bar 41 (performed 93), where
# the home key returns, to close. Broadening only into the hemiola
# cadences that end each half before going on, and a final ritardando.

tempo 150
play 1-12 1-12 13-52 13-52

dyn 1 mf              # A, first time
dyn 13 mp             # A repeat, echo
broaden 23-24 0.93    # the hemiola into the D major cadence, before B
dyn 25 mf             # B, first time (score 13)
broaden 63-64 0.95    # the hemiola at the end of B, before its repeat
dyn 65 mp             # B repeat, echo (score 13)
dyn 93 mf             # score 41 in the repeat: G major returns, full
                      # to the end

rit 2 0.75            # performed 103-104: the last hemiola and fermata
